<Base updated="2026-11-23" title="Home Battery Cost in Pennsylvania (2026): $17,550 Average | HomeBackupCost" description="A 13 kWh home battery averages $17,550 installed in Pennsylvania ($1,350/kWh, EnergySage, Nov 2026). Costs by size, rebate status, outage data, and cheaper options." schema={[{ '@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(([q,a]) => ({ '@type':'Question', name:q, acceptedAnswer:{ '@type':'Answer', text:a } })) }]}>
<h1>How much does a home battery cost in Pennsylvania?</h1>
<p class="lede"><strong>Short answer:</strong> about <strong>$17,550</strong> for a 13 kWh battery installed, before incentives, according to EnergySage marketplace data for Pennsylvania. That's about $1,350 per kWh, above the $1,159/kWh national average. Pennsylvania has no battery rebate we can find, and its state rebate program isn't open yet. As of 2026-11-23.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><div class="mute">Pennsylvania average (13 kWh)</div><div style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--volt)">$17,550</div><div class="src"><a href="https://www.energysage.com/local-data/energy-storage-cost/pa/">EnergySage</a> · updated 2026-11-15</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="mute">Pennsylvania per kWh</div><div style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--volt)">$1,350</div><div class="src">National: $1,159/kWh (<a href="https://www.energysage.com/energy-storage/">EnergySage</a> · updated 2026-08-26)</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="mute">Pennsylvania battery rebate</div><div style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--bad)">None found</div><div class="src"><a href="https://www.pennenergysavers.com/">Penn Energy Savers</a>: coming soon, spring 2027 (checked 2026-11-23)</div></div>
</div>

<h2>Pennsylvania cost by battery size</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>System size</th><th>Average installed cost</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>10 kWh</td><td>$13,500</td></tr><tr><td>13 kWh</td><td>$17,550</td></tr><tr><td>20 kWh</td><td>$27,000</td></tr><tr><td>26 kWh</td><td>$35,100</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.energysage.com/local-data/energy-storage-cost/pa/">EnergySage, Storage cost in Pennsylvania</a>, updated 2026-11-15, checked 2026-11-23. These are quotes on the EnergySage marketplace, before incentives. The math is size × $1,350/kWh: 13 × $1,350 = $17,550. EnergySage gives a 13 kWh range of $14,918–$20,182, and $21,270–$61,000 for solar plus storage. Top quoted models there: Enphase IQ Battery 10C and 5P, FranklinWH aPower2, and Tesla Powerwall 3. EnergySage's separate <a href="https://www.energysage.com/energy-storage/how-much-do-batteries-cost/">cost guide</a> (updated 2026-07-13) lists Pennsylvania at $1,246/kWh for a typical 10 kWh system ($12,460) from first-half-2026 quotes; the local page above is the newer figure.</p>

<h2>Incentives in Pennsylvania</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Federal tax credit:</strong> ended for installs completed after 12/31/2025 (<a href="/federal-tax-credits-ended/">details</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Penn Energy Savers (HOMES and HEEHR, formerly HEAR):</strong> not launched. The program site lists it as coming soon in spring 2027 and describes rebates for high-efficiency appliances and energy-efficiency upgrades for low- and middle-income households, not batteries (<a href="https://www.pennenergysavers.com/">pennenergysavers.com</a>). Pennsylvania DEP says that as of September 2026 it was awaiting final DOE approval to launch (<a href="https://www.pa.gov/agencies/dep/residents/saving-energy/inflation-reduction-act">PA DEP</a>, checked 2026-11-23).</li>
<li><strong>NASEO tracker:</strong> lists Pennsylvania HEAR and HOMES as not launched (<a href="https://www.naseo.org/topics/home-energy-rebates">NASEO</a>, table updated 2026-09-30).</li>
</ul>

<h2>How often Pennsylvania loses power</h2>
<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Pennsylvania, average customer</th><th>2025</th><th>2024</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Minutes without power, all events</td><td>458.1</td><td>336.1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minutes without power, excluding major events</td><td>132.8</td><td>131.0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interruptions per year, all events</td><td>1.408</td><td>1.353</td></tr>
<tr><td>U.S. total, minutes without power, all events</td><td>262.6</td><td>611.3</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/annual/html/epa_11_03.html">EIA Electric Power Annual, Table 11.3</a> (SAIDI and SAIFI), checked 2026-11-23. 458.1 ÷ 60 = 7.6 hours; 458.1 ÷ 262.6 = 1.7 times the U.S. figure. Most of Pennsylvania's 2025 total came from major events (458.1 − 132.8 = 325.3 minutes).</p>

<h2>What it costs to recharge</h2>
<p>Refilling a 13 kWh battery from the grid costs about <strong>$2.83</strong> at Pennsylvania's average residential price of 21.77¢/kWh in August 2026 (U.S. average 18.16¢). The math: 13 × $0.2177 = $2.83, before charging losses. Source: <a href="https://www.eia.gov/electricity/monthly/epm_table_grapher.php?t=epmt_5_6_a">EIA Electric Power Monthly, Table 5.6.A</a>, checked 2026-11-23.</p>

<h2>Cheaper options</h2>
<p>If you mainly need the fridge, furnace blower, sump pump, and Wi-Fi through a short outage, a large portable station with a transfer kit costs much less. For example, an <ProductLink product="EcoFlow DELTA Pro 3" bold={false} button={false} /> ($2,799 sale, standalone unavailable on Nov 16, 2026) plus a Smart Home Panel 2 ($1,499 at ecoflow.com, in stock on Nov 16, 2026) is $4,298 before electrician labor. See <a href="/ecoflow-vs-jackery/">EcoFlow vs Jackery</a>, <a href="/portable-power-stations/">all power stations</a>, <a href="/generac-vs-home-battery/">Generac vs a home battery</a>, and <a href="/standby-generator-installation-cost/">standby generator cost</a>. Pennsylvania winters: see <a href="/winter-storm-power-outage-heat/">keeping the heat on</a>.</p>
<p><a class="btn" href="/calculator/">Size your battery in the calculator</a></p>
<p>Comparing quotes? Use <a href="/how-to-compare-backup-power-quotes/">how to compare quotes</a> and <a href="/questions-to-ask-backup-power-installer/">questions to ask an installer</a>. Next door: <a href="/ohio-home-battery-cost/">home battery cost in Ohio</a>. More states: <a href="/michigan-home-battery-cost/">Michigan</a> · <a href="/indiana-home-battery-cost/">Indiana</a>.</p>
